# Secrets — NEVER commit these
#
# Deny by default. This used to be a list of known-bad filenames, which meant a
# new credential file was committable until someone remembered to add it.
# Ignoring every .txt and naming the safe exceptions inverts the failure mode:
# forget to update this and the new file is ignored, not published.
*.txt
!requirements.txt

.env
*.secret
*_keys/
api_keys/
keys/

# Local identity denylist read by .githooks/pre-commit. Holds the values that
# must never be published (origin IPs, zone/account IDs), so it must never be
# published either.
.identity-denylist

# Data files (accumulated locally, can be large)
data/*.db
data/*.csv
data/*.json
firewall-events-*.json
# data/run_history.jsonl is committed intentionally: an append-only metrics log
# that survives CI cache eviction, and the commit it produces each run keeps the
# repository active enough that GitHub does not auto-disable the schedule at 60
# days. It is committed EMPTY here — structure, not anyone's traffic.

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
.eggs/
venv/
.venv/
env/

# IDE
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Output (regenerated by the pipeline)
output/

# Chart.js, fetched by vendor_chartjs.py rather than committed. Upstream is the
# source of truth: https://github.com/chartjs/Chart.js
lib/
